course: dictionary
lesson: 1739
title: Informal words: clanger
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to chatty, everyday talk rather than to school books. A clanger is one of them. It is informal, so you will hear it among friends more than in formal writing, and it is mainly used in the UK, Australia and New Zealand.
explain: A clanger is a silly, embarrassing mistake, especially something you say that you should not have said. Look for clues in the sentence. If someone says the wrong thing at the wrong moment and everyone goes quiet, that was a clanger. «I dropped a clanger» means I made that kind of mistake.
explain: The usual phrase is «drop a clanger». It is often funny, but it is not wicked, because nobody meant any harm. When you meet this word, ask yourself: was this an awkward mistake that made people wince? If yes, clanger fits.
fact: A clanger is an informal word, so you will hear it in chatty talk more than in formal writing.
fact: A clanger is a silly, embarrassing mistake, often something said at the wrong time.
fact: «Drop a clanger» means to make an embarrassing mistake, as in «I dropped a clanger».
fact: The word is mainly used in the UK, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: Rarely, a clanger can also mean something that makes a loud clanging noise, like a bell clapper.
exercise: Priya reads «I dropped a clanger» in a story. What does clanger mean? | A loud bell ringing in the hall | A long walk home | *A silly, embarrassing mistake | A kind word of thanks
exercise: Sam says the wrong thing at the wrong time and the whole class goes quiet. Which phrase fits what Sam did? | He hung up his coat | He rang the school bell | *He dropped a clanger | He won a prize for reading
exercise: Which phrase means to make an embarrassing mistake? | Pass the salt | Ring a bell | Hang a picture | *Drop a clanger
test: Hannah's mum says, «That was a real clanger, love» after Hannah calls the teacher by the wrong name. What does a real clanger suggest? | Hannah lost her bag | Hannah rang the school bell | Hannah won a prize | *Hannah made an awkward, embarrassing mistake
test: Why is clanger called an informal word? | It is only used in maths lessons | *It is chatty, everyday talk, not formal writing | It is a very old word from Latin | It is a word for a type of tool
exam: Which meaning of clanger is most common in everyday talk? | *A silly, embarrassing mistake | A brave rescue | A loud clanging noise | A type of tool
